# Shared local-development environment. Copy to `.env` at the repository root:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Every service, the Prisma CLI, and the Jest suites read this one file — see
# `packages/config/src/workspace-root.ts` for how it is located (walks up to
# `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, never `process.cwd()`).
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#
#     a real injected environment variable  >  <service>/.env  >  this file
#
# So put anything shared here, and keep a `services/<name>/.env` only for values that genuinely
# belong to one service — `services/orchestrator-svc/.env.example` is the worked example, with the
# AWS Batch resources only it talks to.
#
# Deployed environments have no `.env` at all: the loader finds no workspace root and does nothing,
# leaving the container's injected environment untouched. The deployed variable contract is
# `config/env-manifest.yaml` — every variable, which service reads it, whether it is required, and
# what a missing value costs; that file also holds the reasoning behind the
# values. This file is local development only, and is deliberately much shorter than the manifest:
# most variables have a working default and belong here only when the default is wrong locally.
#
# `pnpm run check:env` fails if a variable retired from the manifest is still set here (ADR-012).
#
# Layout below follows config/env-manifest.yaml's own split: shared groups first (every resident
# service inherits these), then one section per deployable unit for what only it reads.
# services/builder and services/composer are AWS Batch job definitions, not resident services —
# they never read this file at all (see the note in their section, at the bottom).


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Shared — `base` and `postgres` groups: every resident service (verification-svc, orchestrator-svc)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# NODE_ENV, PORT, AWS_REGION, SCVS_DRAIN_GRACE_MS, CORS_ORIGIN (the `base` group) all have working
# defaults for local dev and are not set here. See config/env-manifest.yaml's `base` group if one
# needs overriding — e.g. AWS_REGION only matters once you point SCVS_PUBLISHER_STORAGE at real S3.

# Local Postgres from docker/docker-compose.yml. `@scvs/db` assembles DATABASE_URL from these five
# (packages/db/src/env.ts); set DATABASE_URL directly instead and it wins over all five.
# query-svc does NOT read this block — it has its own database, see the query-svc section below.
POSTGRES_HOST="localhost"
POSTGRES_PORT="5433"
POSTGRES_DB="scvs"
POSTGRES_USER="scvs"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD="scvs_local_dev"
# POSTGRES_SSL="true"   # required against RDS (rds.force_ssl=1 rejects a plain connection); leave
#                        # unset for local docker-compose Postgres, which does not terminate TLS.


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# verification-svc — public HTTP: preflight, submit, job status (SSE), uploads, abuse-guard
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Inherits: base, postgres, stellar-cli, result-storage, verifier-identity. PORT defaults to 5000.

# verification-svc's rate-limit fingerprint is an HMAC of the caller's address under this key, with the
# enforcement window bound into the input (requirement RP-1 of the retention model).
# It has no default: an empty key is the reversible unsalted hash it replaced, so the service refuses to
# boot without one. Deployed environments read it from Secrets Manager; any non-empty string works here.
SCVS_ABUSE_FINGERPRINT_KEY="local-dev-not-a-secret"

# The operator-supplied Terms of Service and Privacy Notice verification-svc serves, and the version a
# submission binds (requirement TA-4: a submission binds the terms version in force). A path relative to the
# workspace root, an absolute path, or an https: URL — `http:` is refused, because a submitter's
# acceptance is recorded against these exact bytes.
#
# It has no usable default and the service refuses to boot without it: this repository ships the assent
# mechanism and nobody's legal text, so there is nothing safe to fall back to. The committed example is
# placeholder text whose `effective_date` is null, which keeps acceptance offered but not required.
# A deployment MUST point this at its own bundle.
SCVS_LEGAL_DOCUMENTS_SOURCE="config/legal-documents.example.json"

# The S3 `staging/` bucket — private source uploads (verification-svc) and the builder's
# `recipe.json` hand-off (orchestrator-svc) both live under it, so it is shared and belongs at the
# top of this file rather than in one service's own section. It is the **scratch** bucket, distinct
# from the published results bucket (SCVS_PUBLISHER_BUCKET below) — `docs/infrastructure-requirements.md`
# §"S3" keeps them apart on purpose. orchestrator-svc fails at BOOT without it; verification-svc
# fails only on the first upload (`POST /v1/uploads/source`), so a healthy verification-svc alone
# proves nothing about this value.
SCVS_STORAGE_BUCKET=scvs-local-scratch

# The `stellar` binary verification-svc's preflight (and orchestrator-svc's chain reader, below)
# spawn. Defaults to `stellar` on PATH.
# STELLAR_CLI_PATH="stellar"

# Scratch directory for CLI workdirs and, with SCVS_PUBLISHER_STORAGE=filesystem, the published
# result tree. Defaults to `$TMPDIR/scvs-artifacts`.
# SCVS_ARTIFACT_DIR=""

# Soroban RPC the `stellar` CLI is pointed at for MAINNET chain lookups. No default — the CLI's
# built-in mainnet network entry is a docs placeholder that refuses with "Bring Your Own". Unset,
# a testnet-only local setup still boots and works; only a mainnet target needs this.
# SCVS_MAINNET_RPC_URL="https://mainnet.sorobanrpc.com"

# ── result-storage: where the published result tree goes ──────────────────────────────────────────
# If you switch SCVS_PUBLISHER_STORAGE to "s3" or "r2" (default is "filesystem", which needs none
# of this), you MUST also set SCVS_R2_REGION explicitly — despite the name, it is the S3 client's
# region for ANY non-filesystem backend, and its default ("auto") is an R2-only literal that real
# AWS S3 rejects outright (`getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND s3.auto.amazonaws.com`). dev uses
# "ap-southeast-2". See config/env-manifest.yaml's SCVS_R2_REGION entry.
# SCVS_PUBLISHER_STORAGE="s3"
# SCVS_PUBLISHER_BUCKET=""
# SCVS_PUBLISHER_PREFIX=""
# SCVS_R2_REGION=""

# Baked IMMUTABLY into every published artifact — a wrong value is not correctable by redeploying,
# only by never having published under it. Defaults to a filesystem URL matching the default
# filesystem backend above; only worth setting if you switch that backend.
# SCVS_PUBLIC_RESULT_ROOT="http://localhost:5000/public"

# ── verifier-identity: who this verifier claims to be in every published artifact ─────────────────
# Defaults to "local-verifier" / "Local Soroban Verifier" / the default policy id below — override
# only if you need published artifacts attributed to something else, e.g. running two local
# verifiers against each other to exercise query-svc's aggregation.
# SCVS_VERIFIER_ID="local-verifier"
# SCVS_VERIFIER_DISPLAY_NAME="Local Soroban Verifier"
# SCVS_VERIFIER_POLICY_ID="sdf-trusted-public-and-private-upload-v1"

# ── verification-svc tunables — every one below has a working default; uncomment only to reproduce
#    a specific rate-limit/retention/timeout issue locally. See config/env-manifest.yaml's
#    `verification-svc` block (`variables` + `tunables`) for the full list and behaviour, including
#    SCVS_UPLOAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE, SCVS_THROTTLE_LIMIT, the two abuse-guard windows
#    (SCVS_ABUSE_MAX_PER_WINDOW / SCVS_SUBMIT_ABUSE_MAX_PER_WINDOW), SCVS_JOB_STREAM_*,
#    SCVS_MAX_SOURCE_ARCHIVE_BYTES, and SCVS_ACTIVE_JOB_DEDUP_STALE_MS.


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# orchestrator-svc — internal only: state machine, AWS Batch dispatch, publish pipeline
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Inherits: base, postgres, result-storage, verifier-identity, verifier-policy, stellar-cli.
# PORT defaults to 5002 (health check only — this service has no other HTTP surface).
#
# ★ orchestrator-svc ALWAYS dispatches builds to real AWS Batch (ADR-003) — there is no in-process
# or local-only fallback. Running it locally means talking to a real AWS account: two Batch job
# queues (one per CPU architecture), matching job-definition families, the scratch S3 bucket above,
# and an SQS queue for Batch's own job-state-change events — plus credentials for all of it. This is
# structural, not a gap in this file: `docs/infrastructure-requirements.md` describes what each
# resource must be able to do, and `services/orchestrator-svc/.env.example` is the worked example
# with the values a local run against the reference AWS account uses. A self-hoster without access
# to that account needs their own equivalent Batch/SQS setup before orchestrator-svc will do
# anything past boot.
#
# The variables below are the required ones (config/env-manifest.yaml's `orchestrator-svc` block);
# see services/orchestrator-svc/.env.example for real values to copy from.

# Two queues, one per architecture (arm: Graviton; x86). A submission's build image is pulled and
# run on that host, so dispatch reads the image's own published architecture and picks the queue
# that can run it — the queue is chosen by the image's architecture. Required, with NO fallback to the
# other architecture when one is unset — that queue cannot run the image, so dispatch throws naming
# the missing variable instead of failing inside the sandbox.
# SCVS_BATCH_JOB_QUEUE_ARN_ARM64="<your-arm64-queue-arn>"
# SCVS_BATCH_JOB_QUEUE_ARN_AMD64="<your-amd64-queue-arn>"

# Which queue takes a build image that runs on BOTH architectures, and the fallback when the
# registry cannot establish an architecture at all. Optional; defaults to arm64.
# SCVS_BATCH_DEFAULT_ARCH="arm64"

# A job-definition FAMILY NAME despite the `_ARN` suffix — SubmitJob resolves the latest
# ACTIVE revision of the family itself. One per architecture, required for the same reason as the
# queue pair above.
# SCVS_BATCH_JOB_DEFINITION_ARN_ARM64="<your-arm64-job-definition-family>"
# SCVS_BATCH_JOB_DEFINITION_ARN_AMD64="<your-amd64-job-definition-family>"

# Routes AWS Batch's own "Job State Change" events (SUCCEEDED/FAILED) to the queue
# BatchEventConsumerService long-polls. Without it no build result is ever consumed — jobs queue and
# dispatch normally and then never complete, and the service boots healthy the whole time. This is
# the highest-consequence `fail: silent` in the system (see this variable's own note in the manifest).
# SCVS_JOB_STATE_CHANGE_QUEUE_URL="<your-sqs-queue-url>"

# ── Composed build environments (ADR-017) — cli-legacy targets only ───────────────────────────────
# All three may be left unset locally: nothing needs them until a cli-legacy target actually reaches
# composition, and ComposerDispatchService then throws naming the missing variable rather than
# leaving the target stuck with no explanation.
# SCVS_COMPOSER_JOB_QUEUE_ARN="<your-composer-queue-arn>"
# SCVS_COMPOSER_JOB_DEFINITION_ARN="<your-composer-job-definition-family>"
# SCVS_COMPOSED_ENVIRONMENT_REPOSITORY_URI="<account>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com/scvs-composed-environments"

# ── Archive backfill ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default mode is "disabled" — nothing archives, no real network calls, no wallet needed. Set
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_MODE to "dry-run" to exercise the durable-pending/wasms.json-append logic with a
# synthetic tx_id and still no network call, or to "irys" for a REAL upload — real uploads default
# to SCVS_ARWEAVE_NETWORK="devnet" (Irys's own test network, free Sepolia-funded test tokens, no
# permanent Arweave record — deleted after ~60 days) and need a real (test) wallet private key.
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_NETWORK="mainnet" spends real ETH and produces a permanent record — never set this
# locally. SCVS_ARWEAVE_GATEWAY_URL must be a real http(s) URL (validated at boot) — do NOT set it
# to an `ar://` scheme URI; that scheme is not directly fetchable, so a client GET against it fails.
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_MODE="irys"
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_NETWORK="devnet"
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_WALLET_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_RPC_URL=""
# SCVS_ARWEAVE_GATEWAY_URL=""
# Independent of SCVS_ARWEAVE_MODE above (requirement MSA-5 of the mainnet source archive): defaults
# to false, so public mainnet source publishes to S3 only. Set to "true" to also archive it.
# SCVS_SOURCE_ARCHIVE_ENABLED="false"

# ── orchestrator-svc tunables — every one below has a working default; uncomment only to reproduce
#    a specific issue locally. See config/env-manifest.yaml's `orchestrator-svc` block for the full
#    list, including SCVS_BUILD_LOG_RETENTION_MS, SCVS_JOB_RETENTION_MS, SCVS_WORKER_CONCURRENCY,
#    the publish/retry/reconciler timers (SCVS_REBUILD_*_TIMEOUT_MS, SCVS_MAX_JOB_RETRY_ATTEMPTS,
#    SCVS_PUBLISH_STALE_MS), and SCVS_MAX_REBUILT_WASM_BYTES.

# ── verifier-policy: content of the published policy.json (read by orchestrator-svc only) ─────────
# Every one of these has a working default matching what was always hardcoded before this config
# surface existed. Leaving them unset changes nothing about what gets published; see
# config/env-manifest.yaml's `verifier-policy` group to publish a different policy locally
# (SCVS_POLICY_ACCEPTED_TRUST_LEVELS, SCVS_POLICY_DIGEST_PINNED_ONLY,
# SCVS_POLICY_TAG_ONLY_ALLOWED, SCVS_POLICY_ARBITRARY_IMAGES_ALLOWED,
# SCVS_POLICY_REQUIRE_TARBALL_SHA256, SCVS_POLICY_RETAIN_ARTIFACTS).


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# query-svc — public HTTP: cross-verifier aggregation over the Trusted Verifiers List
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Inherits: base, query-postgres — NOT the `postgres`/result-storage/verifier-identity groups above.
# It aggregates every verifier's published results over the public Registry API instead of reading
# any verifier's private storage config, and it has its OWN database (see below), independent of
# verification-svc/orchestrator-svc's. PORT defaults to 5001.

# query-svc reads the SAME five POSTGRES_* variable names as the shared block at the top of this
# file (@scvs/db-query re-exports @scvs/db's connection code unchanged) — but they must resolve to
# a DIFFERENT database than verification-svc/orchestrator-svc's once deployed
# (config/env-manifest.yaml's `query-postgres` group; the query service reads its own instance).
#
# Locally there is no organisational-boundary stake, so running query-svc against the SAME
# docker-compose Postgres as the shared block above is harmless and needs nothing further here —
# `pnpm run db:seed-tvl` writes `trusted_verifiers` into whichever database POSTGRES_* resolves to
# when you run it. Only if you want to exercise the two-database separation locally do you need a
# second Postgres and a `services/query-svc/.env` overriding POSTGRES_* for that one service.

# https-only fetch policy that also refuses private, loopback and link-local hosts.
# `config/trusted-verifiers.local.json` — what `pnpm run db:seed-tvl` loads — points at
# http://localhost, so a local run needs this. **Local development only**: with it on, a Trusted
# Verifiers List row can point the aggregator at the instance metadata endpoint.
# SCVS_QUERY_ALLOW_INSECURE_RESULT_ROOTS="true"

# Soroban RPC for mainnet address lookup (`GET /v1/query/mainnet/contracts/...`). No default — SDF
# hosts no public mainnet endpoint, unlike testnet (STELLAR_RPC_URL_TESTNET, which does have one and
# needs no override). Unset, only mainnet address queries fail, at first use rather than at boot.
# STELLAR_RPC_URL_MAINNET="https://mainnet.sorobanrpc.com"

# ── query-svc tunables — every one below has a working default. See config/env-manifest.yaml's
#    `query-svc` block for the full list: SCVS_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS, SCVS_QUERY_CACHE_TTL_MS,
#    SCVS_AGGREGATION_SYNC_INTERVAL_MS, SCVS_BULK_PAGE_LIMIT, SCVS_RPC_TIMEOUT_MS.


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# services/builder and services/composer — NOT configured here, structurally
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Both are AWS Batch job definitions, not resident services with a `dev:*` script — they never run
# as a local process reading this file, and have no `services/<name>/.env.example` for that reason.
#
# Their environment is a JSON document orchestrator-svc injects per job (SCVS_JOB_PARAMS_JSON /
# SCVS_COMPOSER_PARAMS_JSON — see config/env-manifest.yaml's `builder`/`composer` blocks), plus a
# handful of job-definition-level knobs (SCVS_DOCKER_CPUS, SCVS_DOCKER_MEMORY,
# SCVS_PREFETCH_ALLOWED_HOSTS, SCVS_MAX_SOURCE_ARCHIVE_*, ...) that live on the Batch job
# definition itself, never in a `.env` file.
#
# services/builder's env DTO deliberately does NOT extend the shared base DTO (ADR-002): it holds
# no business credentials and no database access, and the manifest's `forbidden:` list for both
# services names every variable that would violate that — POSTGRES_*, DATABASE_URL,
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID/SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, SCVS_PUBLISHER_*, SCVS_STORAGE_BUCKET, SCVS_VERIFIER_ID,
# and both Batch queue/job-definition pairs. This file must never be the thing that hands either of
# them any of that.


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# clients/web
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Vite reads this file (`envDir` in clients/web/vite.config.ts points here), so these belong with
# everything else rather than in a per-package file. Only `VITE_`-prefixed keys reach the bundle.
#
# A `clients/web/.env.local` is read by NOTHING — it looks like the configuration and takes effect
# nowhere. `pnpm run dev:web` warns if it finds one.
#
# Deployed containers use NONE of them: one origin fronts the console and both APIs, so the URLs are
# the empty string baked in at build, and the explorer flag arrives at runtime as
# SCVS_ENABLE_AGGREGATOR on the task definition (docker/web-entrypoint.sh). Locally the two APIs
# are separate ports and no entrypoint substitutes anything, so without these the console sends
# `GET /verifiers` to the Vite dev server and every tab reports "could not be read: Not Found".
#
# Ports are AGENTS.md's defaults; `.claude/launch.json` runs query-svc on 4010 instead, so match
# whichever you actually started.
# VITE_VERIFY_API_URL="http://localhost:5000"
# VITE_QUERY_API_URL="http://localhost:5001"
# VITE_ENABLE_AGGREGATOR="true"
#
# ── debugging against a deployed environment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Instead of the two URLs above, point the dev server at dev or staging and let it proxy `/api`.
# The browser's requests become same-origin, which is both what gets past CORS (a deployed
# environment does not allow `localhost`) and the shape the console actually ships in.
#
# The two URLs MUST be empty when this is set — a non-empty base skips the proxy and fails CORS,
# and an *unset* VITE_VERIFY_API_URL makes the console report itself unconfigured. `dev-proxy.ts`
# refuses to start the server rather than letting either surface later as a 404.
#
# Dev only: `vite build` ignores it and no container sets it.
# VITE_DEV_API_PROXY="https://dev.sv.example.com"
# VITE_VERIFY_API_URL=""
# VITE_QUERY_API_URL=""
